{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'include\\\\(|\\\\.create\\\\(|\\\\.close\\\\(' prisma-8.md node_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres -g '*.md' | head -70\"","aggregated_output":"node_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/0.15-to-0.16/instructions.md:23:      `contract infer`'s back-relation field name generation used a hand-rolled pluralization rule that appended `es` to any table name already ending in `s`/`x`/`z`/`ch`/`sh`, doubling an already-plural table name (`sessions` -> `sessionses`). `contract infer` now uses real inflection (the `pluralize` library) and produces the correct name (`sessions` stays `sessions`; a genuinely singular `status` still becomes `statuses`). This only affects a future `contract infer` run — an already-generated `.prisma` file is untouched, so nothing breaks until you next re-run infer. If you do re-run `contract infer` against a database with an already-plural table name, diff the regenerated `.prisma` file for any back-relation field whose name changed — that's a public field name your application code accesses via `.include()`/`.select()`/the generated TypeScript types, so update those call sites to the corrected name.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:15:- User is running a one-off script (`tsx my-script.ts`, Node CLI, CI task) and the process won't exit after queries finish, or they need script teardown (`db.close()`, `await using`).\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:66:The Mongo façade has the same construction shape — `import mongo from '@internal/mongo/runtime'` — and the same `db.connect(...)` / `db.close()` lifecycle methods. **The Mongo façade does not expose `db.transaction(...)`.** See *What Prisma 8 doesn't do yet* for the workaround. **The ORM surface differs in one place: keys.** On Mongo, `db.orm` is keyed by the collection's storage name (from `@@map(...)`, or the lowercased model name if no `@@map` is set), not by the PSL model name — so `model User { … @@map(\"users\") }` is reached at `db.orm.users`, not `db.orm.public.User`. The SQL builder lane (`db.sql.<ns>.<table>`) doesn't exist on Mongo at all (`db.sql` is `undefined`). See `references/queries.md` § *MongoDB ORM addressing* for the full rule and a rewrite recipe for SQL-target examples.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:70:The concept: short scripts that connect, query, then expect the process to exit will **hang on Postgres** because the `pg.Pool` owned by `postgres()` keeps Node's event loop alive. The data round-trip succeeds; the script never exits. Call `await db.close()` before the script returns (or use `await using` **at the top of a script module** so teardown runs when the module exits — see the block-scope warning below for why this matters).\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:78:const created = await db.orm.public.User.create({ email: 'alice@example.com', name: 'Alice' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:82:await db.close();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:96:// db.close() runs automatically when the script module exits.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:129:Servers (HTTP handlers, workers in a request loop) **do not call `db.close()`** at all in steady state. The pool stays open for the process lifetime. `db.close()` and `await using` are for short-lived scripts — `tsx my-script.ts`, Node CLI commands, CI tasks, one-off seed runs — not for code that runs inside a request loop.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:136:- **Ownership.** `close()` releases only what the façade constructed (`pg.Pool` from `{ url }`, `MongoClient` from `{ url }` / `{ uri, dbName }`, SQLite handle from `{ path }`). If you supplied your own `pg.Pool` / `pg.Client` (Postgres `pg:` option), `mongodb.MongoClient` (Mongo `mongoClient:` option), or a pre-built `binding`, `db.close()` does **not** touch those — you own their lifecycle.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:138:**`db.end()` does not exist.** The universal `node-postgres` name is `pool.end()` on a `pg.Pool`; the Prisma 8 runtime client is not a `pg.Pool`. The right call is `await db.close()`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:166:The rule to remember: inside a request, `db` does everything the `db` from `postgres()` does, so any documented `db.orm...`, `db.sql...`, `db.raw...`, `db.transaction(...)`, `db.prepare(...)` or `db.query(...)` snippet works unchanged. Await every query before the `await using` scope ends. The connection's `close()` waits until the runtime has been idle for one turn of the event loop (a `setTimeout(0)`), then refuses new work. A lazy ORM read such as `db.orm.public.User.all()`, or `db.query(plan)`, returned from the scope without `await` starts only when the caller awaits it, after the scope has closed, so it rejects with `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED` (\"Runtime is closed\"), whose `fix` names the missing `await`. Work that keeps the runtime busy from the close onward completes: a `first()`, `execute()` or `transaction()`, an ORM write and its reload, including `include(...).create()` and a nested `create()`, and an async helper whose steps go through `db.orm`, `db.transaction()` or `db.prepare()`; a transaction commits. Such work is never refused because of the close; if it fails for its own reason, it rejects like any unawaited promise. A helper that calls `db.runtime()` after the scope has ended throws \"Postgres connection is closed\" at that call. Work that starts after the runtime was idle for a turn is refused. Whether a query sent after waiting on something other than the database counts as idle depends on when that callback runs relative to the timer, so do not rely on it. An unawaited promise that fails then, or fails for its own reason, rejects like any unawaited promise, and Node reports it as unhandled. Write `return await db.orm...`, not `return db.orm...`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:173:- **After `db.close()`** (or the end of the `await using` scope), `db.runtime()` throws `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED` (\"Postgres connection is closed\"), and ORM queries, `db.transaction(...)` and prepared statements that start after the runtime was idle for a turn reject with `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED` (\"Runtime is closed\"). Call `postgres.connect({ url })` again for a new connection.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:364:  const user = await tx.orm.public.User.create({ email: 'alice@example.com' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:365:  await tx.orm.public.Post.create({ userId: user.id, title: 'hello' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:415:`path` is optional at construct time (you can call `db.connect({ path })` later); omit it and the façade still returns a client. The SQLite façade exposes the same `db.sql`, `db.orm`, `db.raw`, `db.prepare(...)`, `db.transaction(...)`, `db.close()`, and `[Symbol.asyncDispose]` surfaces as Postgres, with one addressing difference: SQLite has no schemas, so `db.sql` and `db.orm` are the unbound namespace itself (`db.orm.User`, `db.sql.user`) instead of Postgres's `db.orm.public.User` / `db.sql.public.user`. `db.orm` and `tx.orm` also offer `fragment` beside the models, unless a model is named `fragment`: `const titled = db.orm.fragment({ title: { codecId: 'sqlite/text@1', nullable: false } }, (rows) => rows.where((r) => r.title.eq('Hello')));` then `await db.orm.Post.with(titled).all();`. The Mongo façade shares `db.orm`, `db.close()`, and `[Symbol.asyncDispose]` but has no `db.sql` and no `db.transaction(...)`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:437:8. **Script hangs after queries finish on Postgres.** The `pg.Pool` keeps Node's event loop alive. Solution: `await db.close()` before the script returns, or `await using db = postgres({ contract, ... })` at the top of a script module. Do not put `await using db = postgres(...)` inside a request handler — it's block-scoped and would close the pool after every request. The right server pattern is a module-level singleton in `db.ts` that lives for the process lifetime. On a per-request runtime, use `postgresServerless()` and `await using db = await postgres.connect({ url })` in the handler instead (see *Workflow — Serverless and per-request runtimes*).\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:21:| Eager-load a reference relation | **ORM `.include('<relation>')`** | Lowers to `$lookup`; composes with `.where` / `.select` / `.orderBy` / `.limit`. |\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:71:Mongo reference relations eager-load through the same `.include('<relation>')` surface; the ORM lowers to `$lookup`:\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:75:  .include('author')\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:89:const user = await db.orm.users.create({\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/0.16-to-0.17/instructions.md:276:      relation `.include()` over a date column now decodes instead of failing with\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/0.16-to-0.17/instructions.md:429:      `.include()`'s nested rows, an aggregated child row set — projects each column through its\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/0.16-to-0.17/instructions.md:499:      A `sqlite/json@1` column read through a nested `.include()` arrives as a parsed document\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.17-to-8.0.0-rc.18/instructions.md:571:A state built by hand for an include child must not reuse the parent's table aliases. Prefer building includes through `collection.include(...)` and reading `collection.state`, which aliases the child's tables from the parent's scope. Where a child state is built by hand for a relation whose target table already appears in the parent chain (a self-relation, or an include that returns to an ancestor's table), build it through `include()` instead; `createCollectionTables` starts a fresh scope and would give the child the same alias as its parent.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.17-to-8.0.0-rc.18/instructions.md:616:This affects only an `IncludeExpr` object written out by hand with a `through` descriptor. An include built with `collection.include('<relation>')` carries the aliased junction table already.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.17-to-8.0.0-rc.18/instructions.md:625:const [include] = users.include('tags').state.includes;\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/0.15-to-0.16/instructions.md:25:      `contract infer`'s back-relation field name generation used a hand-rolled pluralization rule that appended `es` to any table name already ending in `s`/`x`/`z`/`ch`/`sh`, doubling an already-plural table name (`sessions` -> `sessionses`). `contract infer` now uses real inflection (the `pluralize` library) and produces the correct name (`sessions` stays `sessions`; a genuinely singular `status` still becomes `statuses`). This only affects a future `contract infer` run — an already-generated `.prisma` file is untouched, so nothing breaks until you next re-run infer for your pack. If you do re-run `contract infer` against a schema with an already-plural table name, diff the regenerated `.prisma` file for any back-relation field whose name changed — that's a public field name your pack's consumers access via `.include()`/`.select()`/the generated TypeScript types, so a rename is a breaking change to your pack's own published surface, to be versioned and documented like any other.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:21:| Eager-load related records | **ORM `.include(...)`** | Composes with `.where` / `.select` / `.orderBy` / `.limit` per branch. |\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:133:  .include('posts', (posts) => posts.using.post_search(q).limit(3))\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:257:The concept: `.include('<relation>', (branch) => branch.<chain>)` adds a relation branch to the parent query. The branch is its own collection — compose `.where` / `.select` / `.orderBy` / `.limit` on it just like the parent.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:262:  .include('posts', (post) =>\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:273:**Reduce a to-many relation to a scalar.** A refinement callback may return a *reducer* — `count()`, `sum(field)`, `avg(field)`, `min(field)`, `max(field)`, plus the lossless `countBigInt()`, `sumBigInt(field)`, and `avgDecimal(field)` — instead of a collection. The parent's relation field then carries that one value rather than an array. Reducers exist only inside an `include(...)` callback; calling one elsewhere throws.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:276:await db.orm.public.User.include('posts', (posts) => posts.count()).all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:279:await db.orm.public.User.include('posts', (posts) => posts.sum('views')).all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:282:await db.orm.public.User.include('posts', (posts) => posts.avg('views')).all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:285:await db.orm.public.User.include('posts', (posts) => posts.min('views')).all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:286:await db.orm.public.User.include('posts', (posts) => posts.max('views')).all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:290:await db.orm.public.User.include('posts', (posts) => posts.sumBigInt('views')).all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:294:await db.orm.public.User.include('posts', (posts) =>\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:308:const user = await db.orm.public.User.create({ id, email, displayName, kind, createdAt });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:313:  .create({ id, email, displayName, kind, createdAt });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:386:Class methods chain with each other and with the built-in methods, in any order, and after `.include(...)`:\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:391:await Post.include('user').newestFirst().all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:396:Inside a class body, a class method called on the result of another call loses what that call established. So a class method whose body chains two class methods loses the first call's facts for every caller: with `latest() { return this.byAuthor(id).newestFirst(); }`, `Post.latest()` is known to be ordered but not filtered. The same holds for `.prepared` after `.include(...)` inside the class: it describes the class's row without the included relation. Inside the class, follow a class method with built-in methods (`this.byAuthor(id).orderBy(...)`), or chain the class methods from outside the class, where they keep every fact.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:454:  posts.select('id', 'title', 'createdAt').include('tags'),\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:459:await db.orm.public.User.include('posts', (posts) => posts.with(postSummary)).all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:618:  const user = await tx.orm.public.User.create({ id, email });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:619:  await tx.orm.public.Post.create({ userId: user.id, title: 'hello' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:666:const user = await db.orm.public.User.create({ id: 1, email: 'a@x.io' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:667:const authUser = await db.orm.auth.User.create({ id: 2, token: 'tok' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:672:Cross-namespace relations (e.g. `public.Profile` → `auth.User`) follow the same `.include()` syntax; the ORM resolves the correct schema-qualified join automatically.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/references/supabase.md:125:Options beyond the basics: `middleware` (same composition as `postgres()` — see `references/runtime.md`; middleware never sees the role-binding `set_config` calls), `poolOptions`, `pg` (BYO `pg.Pool` instead of `url`; a `pg.Client` is rejected with `SUPABASE.CONFIG_INVALID`). Teardown is `await db.close()` / `await using` exactly as in `references/runtime.md` — the same script-hang rules apply.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.1-to-8.0.0-rc.2/instructions.md:102:      `aggregate()`, `groupBy().aggregate()`, `groupBy().having()`, the `include(...)` reducers,\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.1-to-8.0.0-rc.2/instructions.md:142:      `RUNTIME.DECODE_FAILED` instead of returning a rounded number — on the `.include()` path as\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.1-to-8.0.0-rc.2/instructions.md:163:        - \".include(\"\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.1-to-8.0.0-rc.2/instructions.md:289:| `db.orm.User.include('posts', (posts) => posts.count())` | one reducer per declared operation |\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.1-to-8.0.0-rc.2/instructions.md:406:That is the trade these defaults make: a value you can compare, serialise, and do arithmetic with, and a loud failure rather than a quietly wrong total. It fires on the `.include()` path too — the reducer's value travels as a JSON number, but the guard runs after the parse, and rounding is monotone, so a value that was outside the range is still outside it after parsing.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:263:      On a client that closes at once (`postgres({ contract, url })`, `sqlite()`, `supabase()`), an ORM call that resolves to one value, such as `first()`, `create()`, `include(...).create()` or a nested create, starts when it is called, and `close()` waits for it, so one left pending at `close()` now completes instead of rejecting: a `create()` used to reject with \"Runtime is closed\", and `include(...).create()` and a nested create with \"Postgres client is closed\". A streaming read such as `all()` starts when it is read; read after `close()`, it rejects with \"Postgres client is closed\" instead of \"Runtime is closed\". Update code or tests that expect the old outcome.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:270:      A contract built with `defineContract` from TypeScript now types each relation with its target model, namespace and cardinality, as an emitted `contract.d.ts` does. Rows from `include(...)`, which were typed `unknown` and always as an array, now have the target model's field types: a has-many or many-to-many relation is an array, a belongs-to relation is one row (or `null` when optional), and a has-one relation is one row or `null`. A cast on an included value that disagrees with this type now fails to typecheck. The detection matches a file that imports `defineContract` from a SQL contract builder; the change matters only where code queries through the contract it builds without an emitted `contract.d.ts`.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1026:await db.orm.public.Post.create({ title: 'Hello', publishedAt: new Date() as unknown as string });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1029:await db.orm.public.Post.create({ title: 'Hello', publishedAt: new Date().toISOString() });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1377:  const post = await db.orm.Post.include('author').first();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1398:The same holds for reducers inside `include(...)`, such as `posts.count()`. The aggregates come from the aggregate descriptors of the target, the adapter and every extension, as `prisma contract emit` collects them. Aggregates contributed only by the execution stack, not by a pack in the contract, are still not in the contract's types and still need the cast.\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1426:- await db.orm.User.create({ id: '123e4567-e89b-12d3-a456-426614174000' as Char<36>, email });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1428:+ await db.orm.User.create({ id: '123e4567-e89b-12d3-a456-426614174000', email });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1550:const user = await db.orm.users.create({ name: 'Alice' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1552:const posts = await db.orm.posts.where({ authorId: user._id }).include('author').all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1555:const user = await db.orm.users.create({ name: 'Alice' });\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1557:const posts = await db.orm.posts.where({ authorId: user.id }).include('author').all();\nnode_modules/@prisma/orm-postgres/skills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.20-to-8.0.0-rc.21/instructions.md:326:await db.close();\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
